{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-956e7e6a91f2f55c", "type": "tool_result", "content": "100\tcompared to smaller, targeted models [25], a finding that persists in the symbolic domain [43, 19].\n101\tSystems that close the gap do so by attaching domain-specific perception modules to the LLM [41],\n102\tthe same role the “ears” play in our system.\n103\tGenerative music models implicitly encode musical structure and style [6, 12, 28], but implicit\n104\tencoding is not articulation: a co-writing partner must say what structures are present and what\n105\tchanges might improve a composition or arrangement. Machine agents could exchange feedback as\n106\tmathematical signals, e.g. as gradients toward some endpoint, as in classifier guidance [10], but a\n107\thuman collaborator cannot act on a gradient. Description and reproduction make different demands:\n108\trepresentation learning and reconstruction are separate tasks, long regarded as a tradeoff [11].\n109\tRepresentation autoencoders (RAEs) [44] show the tradeoff is an artifact of compression: their latents\n110\trearrange the input at nearly its original dimensionality, with reconstruction handled separately rather\n111\tthan by the representation objective. The pairing supports discriminative tasks and high-quality\n112\tgeneration alike. We adopt this paradigm for music, with a conditional flow model standing in for the\n113\ttrained decoder: a single encoder, trained with no reconstruction objective and then frozen, serving\n114\tas the ears and conditioning the mouth.\n115\tThe internal representations are formed by a joint-embedding predictive architecture (JEPA) [20, 1].\n116\tJEPA has been applied to music before: Stem-JEPA [32] predicted musical stem compatibility\n117\tfrom audio; Hachana and Rasheed [15] adapted JEPA to tokenized symbolic music with musically\n118\tmotivated masking of instruments, pitch classes and octaves, and found the learned representation\n119\tdominated by positional information, which limited transfer to content tasks such as genre and style;\n120\tand, concurrently with this work, Music-JEPA [38] learned an action-conditioned audio world model\n121\tfrom paired piano audio and performance data, while ARIMA [42] learned windowed latent-predictive\n122\trepresentations of symbolic music. Unlike these, our approach operates purely on symbolic (MIDI)\n123\tpiano-roll images, is trained with a hierarchical, multi-level Swin V2 encoder rather than a single-scale\n124\tbackbone, prevents representational collapse via a LeJEPA-style distributional regularizer (SIGReg)\n125\trather than an EMA teacher, and makes shift structure explicit in the objective rather than leaving it\n126\tto be absorbed by positional encodings. This paper extends our earlier report [17], which details the\n127\tarchitecture and training objectives; here we focus on the musical and co-creative capabilities, and\n128\treport improvements to the model and the generative pipeline together with an assessment of gains\n129\tfrom supervised signals and additional datasets.\n130\tTo maximize accessibility for GPU-poor musicians (who may also prefer not to send works-inprogress to a cloud service) we adopted a fundamental engineering constraint: timely inference on\n131\tCPU-only runtimes, demonstrated by a live demo linked from the upplemental materials.1\n132\t1 Supplemental Materials: drscotthawley.github.io/midi-rae-jepa-son\n133\t\n134\t2\n135\t\n136\t\fFigure 2: Just as a vision world model pans and tilts a camera to scan a panoramic scene, we take\n137\tsquare crops of MIDI piano-roll images and compare crops translated in time by ∆x time steps\n138\tor in pitch by ∆y semitones. Given one crop and a shift vector (∆x, ∆y), the model predicts\n139\tthe embedding of the shifted crop. Regions outside the image are filled with blanks. Our design\n140\trequirement of fast CPU execution fixes the crop at 128×128 pixels. Pixels are binary, so note\n141\tvelocity is discarded, which is acceptable for the songwriting use case. Time is quantized to 32nd\n142\tnotes on a tempo-normalized grid, the finest grid at which a blank frame can separate two repeated\n143\tnotes in a binary image, so a crop spans four bars in 4/4, or eight seconds at 120 BPM.\n144\tL5\n145\t\n146\tL4\n147\t\n148\tL3\n149\t\n150\tL2\n151\t\n152\tL1\n153\t\n154\tL0\n155\t\n156\t4×4 patches\n157\t\n158\t8×8 patches\n159\t\n160\t16×16 patches\n161\t\n162\t32×32 patches\n163\t\n164\t64×64 patches\n165\t\n166\t128×128 patch\n167\t\n168\t(a)\n169\t\n170\t(b)\n171\t\n172\t2× ↓\n173\t\n174\t32×32\n175\t\n176\tdepth=2\n177\tdim=8\n178\theads=2\n179\t\n180\t2× ↓\n181\t\n182\t2× ↓\n183\t\n184\tdepth=2\n185\t\n186\tdepth=2\n187\tdim=32\n188\theads=2\n189\t\n190\t16×16 dim=16\n191\t\n192\t8×8\n193\t\n194\t2× ↓\n195\t\n196\tdepth=6\n197\t\n198\t4×4 dim=64\n199\theads=4\n200\t\n201\t2× ↓\n202\t\n203\tdepth=2\n204\t\n205\t2×2 dim=128\n206\theads=8\n207\t\n208\tdepth=2\n209\t\n210\t1×1 dim=256\n211\theads=16\n212\t\n213\theads=2\n214\t\n215\tFigure 3: Our 2.55 million-parameter hierarchical Swin V2 Encoder. Model architecture hyperparameters were selected via ablation studies detailed in the Supplemental Materials (Appendices A–F).\n216\t\n217\t2\n218\t\n219\tHow: A Symbolic Music “World Model”\n220\t“It wasn’t made by people who went to the music conservatory. It was made by kids who felt\n221\tsomething.”\n222\t—Rick Rubin, on early hip-hop [35]\n223\t\n224\tRather than relying on supervised labels and the vocabulary of music theory, we train a self-supervised\n225\tmodel that builds its own (hierarchical) representations of music, its own ‘feel’ for musical structure.\n226\tIt does this by exploring the “space” of musical compositions, cf. Figure 2. Human musical\n227\trepresentations are hierarchical: notes, chords, phrases, song structure. Yet labeled data for that\n228\thierarchy is limited. Chord detectors and song-structure predictors can supply some of it, but we\n229\twant to learn the hierarchy without labels, the way World Models in computer vision [14, 13] build\n230\tsemantic representations by predicting embeddings of one view of a scene from another. We do this\n231\ton piano-roll images, partly to take advantage of the Swin V2 transformer [23, 22], a hierarchical\n232\trepresentation learner already proven on images, and partly because a piano roll is a semantic proxy\n233\tfor an audio spectrogram, with perceptually aligned axes. Latents trained for reconstruction accuracy\n234\trarely become semantically meaningful; they tend instead to resemble spatially downsampled copies\n235\tof the input. Following the RAE paradigm, ours is a latent model with no compression: the latents hold\n236\tnearly as many numbers as the input (16,384 pixels → 16,128 floats), rearranged into a meaningful\n237\tgeometry rather than a smaller one.\n238\tTraining objective. The encoder is trained with a combined self-supervised objective,\n239\tL = λLequiv + (1 − λ)LSIGReg + λMEP LMEP + λfact Lfact ,\n240\tapplied level-wise. Lequiv is an equivariance loss that pulls together or pushes apart embeddings\n241\tof shifted crop pairs in proportion to the shift magnitude, so the latent space respects pitch/time\n242\ttranslation structure rather than collapsing to shift-invariance. LSIGReg is LeJEPA’s sketched isotropic\n243\tGaussian regularization [2], which applies an Epps–Pulley test along random projections to enforce\n244\tan isotropic Gaussian prior, preventing representational collapse. (We also tried VISReg [39], which\n245\t3\n246\t\n247\t\freplaces the Epps–Pulley test with a sliced-Wasserstein distance, and saw no consistent improvement\n248\tin our metrics.) The equivariance and SIGReg losses are applied only at L0–L3: at L4 and L5,\n249\tSIGReg washes out the fine note-level structure those levels carry, and patch-level equivariance is less\n250\tmeaningful at those scales. LMEP is an intra- and inter-level masked-embedding-prediction loss in the\n251\tstyle of I-JEPA [1]: an auxiliary predictor infers an EMA teacher’s embeddings from the student’s full\n252\tcontext. Lfact is a soft factorization loss which pulls pitch- and time-shift difference vectors toward\n253\tparallel, anti-parallel, or orthogonal geometry depending on augmentation type. It is applied only at\n254\tL0–L2, enforcing equivariance out to larger shifts than Lequiv achieves alone [17]. Full derivations\n255\tand hyperparameters are given in the Supplemental Materials (Appendices A–F).\n256\t\n257\t3\n258\t\n259\tEars: What Does It Actually “Perceive”?\n260\t\n261\tThe “ears” of a full system such as that diagrammed in Figure 1 can partially consist of libraries\n262\tof lightweight audio feature detectors, predicting categories such as genre, instrument type, and\n263\tmood [4], or metrics such as pitch and tempo [27]. In addition, independent detectors for chords,\n264\tkeys, phrase boundaries and song structure are available. For songwriting, we are concerned less with\n265\tnuances of performance than with the notes the player intended. Discarding audio detail in favor of a\n266\tstructured MIDI representation therefore costs us little. Audio-to-MIDI conversion [3] recovers that\n267\tintent in a compact, semantic form already familiar from many music creators’ workflows, and recent\n268\timprovements [34] have made it reliable enough to build on. We treat it as upstream of this work.\n269\tOur encoder is not meant to replace those small detectors. It learns representations that resist easy\n270\tlabeling, its own sense of “feel,” obtained only by exploring the space of musical compositions. To\n271\tgauge what those representations correspond to musically, we turn to standard probes. Each probe\n272\tbelow trains a small linear (or ridge) model on frozen, per-level embeddings, or measures a geometric\n273\tproperty of the embedding space directly; none fine-tune the encoder.\n274\tWe call the model introduced here MIDI-RAE-JEPA-SON (MRJS), a successor of our earlier\n275\treport’s MIDI-RAE-JEPA [17], which appears in Table 1 as MRJ-48∧ (∆tmax =48, factorization\n276\tloss active), alongside a DINOv2 [29] baseline and a chance column ( ). Unlike MRJ-48∧, whose\n277\tpipeline components were trained against differing train/test splits on a dataset since found to contain\n278\tcorruption, MRJS and every downstream component share a single unified split on corrected data.\n279\tThat column is therefore historical reference, not a controlled comparison. The table reports each\n280\tprobe at the hierarchy level where it scores best, and those levels carry as much information as the\n281\tscores. Phrase boundaries peak at the coarse end (L0–L2) while note density and harmonic content\n282\tpeak at the fine end (L4–L5), so the level at which a property becomes decodable tracks the musical\n283\tTable 1: Probing frozen embeddings for musical structure. Each cell reports a model’s best value\n284\tacross its hierarchy levels (minimum for ↓ metrics), with the winning level in small type and a bar\n285\twhose height is proportional to that level’s receptive-field size (L0 tallest); = chance level for that\n286\tmetric; bold = best model per row; cells are shaded per row with the plasma colormap (yellow =\n287\tbest). Cross-song: whether same-song embeddings sit closer together than cross-song ones. Time\n288\tR2 : how well the distance between two embeddings predicts the temporal offset between their crops.\n289\tJoint Chord: root and chord quality jointly. Chroma R2 : recovery of the local 12-bin pitch-class\n290\tdistribution. Phrase AP/AUC: linear probe detecting whether a human-annotated phrase boundary\n291\tfalls within half a bar of a crop’s center, scored as average precision and ROC-AUC.\n292\tMetric\n293\t\n294\tDINOv2 MRJ-48∧ MRJS +chords +phrases Lakh-1× Lakh-4×\n295\t2\n296\t\n297\tNote Density R ↑\n298\tCross-song ↓\n299\tTime R2 ↑\n300\tRoot Note ↑\n301\tJoint Chord ↑\n302\tKey Detection ↑\n303\tChroma R2 ↑\n304\tPhrase AP ↑\n305\tPhrase AUC ↑\n306\t\n307\tn/a\n308\tn/a\n309\tn/a\n310\t.08\n311\t.05\n312\t.05\n313\t.00\n314\t.17\n315\t.50\n316\t\n317\t.93 L1\n318\t.68 L1\n319\t.23 L1\n320\t.24 L0\n321\t.17 L1\n322\t.19 L0\n323\t.36 L1\n324\t.21 L0\n325\t.57 L0\n326\t\n327\t.93 L5\n328\t.69 L5\n329\t.24 L2\n330\t.25 L5\n331\t.17 L5\n332\t.18 L5\n333\t.54 L4\n334\t.28 L1\n335\t.61 L2\n336\t\n337\t.88 L5\n338\t.80 L3\n339\t.26 L4\n340\t.24 L5\n341\t.18 L5\n342\t.16 L5\n343\t.65 L5\n344\t.27 L0\n345\t.61 L0\n346\t\n347\t4\n348\t\n349\t.91 L5\n350\t.73 L1\n351\t.24 L1\n352\t.59 L2\n353\t.54 L3\n354\t.70 L3\n355\t.83 L4\n356\t.25 L2\n357\t.58 L0\n358\t\n359\t.89 L5\n360\t.57 L3\n361\t.24 L2\n362\t.22 L5\n363\t.18 L5\n364\t.17 L5\n365\t.75 L5\n366\t.29 L2\n367\t.62 L0\n368\t\n369\t.94 L5\n370\t.62 L4\n371\t.25 L2\n372\t.22 L5\n373\t.18 L5\n374\t.15 L5\n375\t.76 L5\n376\t.28 L1\n377\t.61 L1\n378\t\n379\t.94 L5\n380\t.77 L5\n381\t.22 L3\n382\t.26 L5\n383\t.20 L5\n384\t.18 L5\n385\t.61 L5\n386\t.26 L0\n387\t.61 L0\n388\t\n389\t\ftime scale on which it operates. Temporal offset is the weakest probe throughout, .22–.26 for every\n390\tmodel, which is expected: a piano roll is nearly stationary along the time axis, so sliding a crop\n391\tsideways yields more of the same, whereas pitch is absolute and a vertical shift moves material into a\n392\tdifferent register. We read the harmony scores as capacity probes, not as downstream performance;\n393\tsmall dedicated chord detectors do that job far better. The DINOv2 baseline is instructive: on\n394\tproperties that reduce to image statistics it is competitive or better, reaching .93 on note density\n395\tand separating songs more sharply than MRJS, but it falls away on the musically specific probes,\n396\trecovering chroma at .36 against .65 and phrase boundaries at .21 AP against .27.\n397\tTo assess the effects of dataset variation and size, we trained encoders on subsets of the Lakh MIDI\n398\tDataset [31] containing 1× and 4× as many songs as POP909. Both transfer well: on the POP909\n399\tprobes they match or exceed MRJS on note density and cross-song separation. At matched training\n400\tbudget, however, the 4× subset gave no consistent gain over 1×, and degraded chroma (.76 to .61)\n401\tand cross-song separation (.62 to .77). We also explored adding auxiliary supervised objectives to the\n402\tself-supervised recipe: chord recognition, using POP909’s chord annotations, and phrase-boundary\n403\tprediction, using the human-verified annotations of Dai et al. [9], each applied as a small linear head\n404\ton the coarse levels during training. Chord supervision makes harmonic content linearly decodable\n405\twhere it was barely present: root identification rises from .24 to .59, joint chord from .18 to .54, and\n406\tkey detection, which was never supervised, from .16 to .70. It also moves the seat of that information\n407\tfrom L5 (near note-level) to the coarser and more “abstract” L2–L3. The cost is small: phrase AP\n408\tfalls from .27 to .25 and temporal offset from .26 to .24, while note density and cross-song separation\n409\tboth improve. Phrase supervision helps less, but is not without effect: boundary detection rises only\n410\tslightly (AP .27 to .29, AUC .61 to .62), while cross-song separation improves markedly (.80 to .57).\n411\tHarmonic content, then, has to be asked for; temporal and phrase structure largely emerge from the\n412\tself-supervised objectives alone. Once the inputs are encoded, they can be sent to the “brain” as well\n413\tas used as conditioning signals for the mouth described next.\n414\t\n415\t4\n416\t\n417\tMouth: Making Suggestions\n418\t\n419\tVerbal feedback and lyrical suggestions can be handled by a properly harnessed LLM. Musical\n420\tsuggestions benefit from their own generative model conditioned on the idea the user already has,\n421\tas in an inpainting task where we replace a melody or an accompaniment, or suggest a different\n422\tcontinuation. Hachana and Rasheed [15] anticipate that a reliable JEPA music representation could\n423\tenable generative applications; this section realizes one. Autoregressive transformers generate left\n424\tto right, so filling a hole in the middle of an existing passage is not something they do natively, and\n425\tmust be trained for specifically [36]. Diffusion models over piano-roll images, by contrast, support\n426\tinpainting at inference time essentially for free [28, 16], and in a form that admits graphical prompts:\n427\tthe user draws on the piano roll where they want new notes, and the model generates notes suiting the\n428\tsurrounding material. But the price of this power is inference cost. Diffusion inpainting needs many\n429\tintegration steps, and our own earlier system, Pictures of MIDI [16], was large and slow enough\n430\tto require a GPU. That is disqualifying under our design constraint. We therefore moved to flow\n431\tmatching [21], which can generate in very few integration steps: the smooth flow admits higher-order\n432\tintegration schemes such as RK4, and optimal-transport pairing [37] during training straightens the\n433\ttrajectories, making even Euler steps sufficient.\n434\tRecent work has favored flowing in pixel space rather than in the latent space [8, 24]. We do the same:\n435\twe flow from noise in pixel space, conditioned on embeddings of the user’s existing musical idea. We\n436\tsaid earlier that there is no compression in the embeddings; however, we find it sufficient to reduce the\n437\tconditioning signal via PCA, keeping at least 90% of the variance per level, resulting in a reduction\n438\tof over 3× (16,128 → 4,835 floats). This still reconstructs the input closely (F1=0.996), yet the goal\n439\tis variations rather than copies. How far those variations stray is controllable: guidance strength sets\n440\thow tightly a sample follows its conditioning, and dropping conditioning at different levels loosens it\n441\tfurther. That dropout also points to how we handle inpainting. To inpaint, we apply spatial dropout\n442\tto the PCA conditioning at the locations the user painted. The flow was trained with exactly this\n443\tdropout, so it fills the gap from surrounding context without needing an inpainting-specific sampler,\n444\tunlike sampling-time schemes for diffusion models [33], and we adopt none of the flow-specific\n445\tschemes [30, 26]. Varying the dropout probability per level sets how coarse or fine the replacement\n446\tis: dropping only the fine levels rewrites notes while keeping the harmonic frame, dropping every\n447\tlevel rewrites the passage.\n448\t5\n449\t\n450\t\fEncoder\n451\t\n452\tPCA\n453\t\n454\tDropout\n455\t\n456\tInput\n457\tFlow\n458\tHand-drawn mask\n459\t\n460\tNoise\n461\t\n462\tVariations - 4 seeds\n463\t(actual data)\n464\t\n465\tFigure 4: Unified generation and inpainting. The encoder’s PCA-reduced conditioning maps pass\n466\tthrough a dropout stage that zeroes patches under a hand-drawn mask, at user-chosen strengths per\n467\tlevel; a flow-matching model then generates a window from noise, guided only by the surviving\n468\tconditioning (10 Euler steps, guidance 1). Outside the mask the output is overwritten with the input,\n469\tpreserving untouched music exactly. With no mask, the same pipeline performs ordinary conditioned\n470\tgeneration, varied by seed or guidance strength. The piano rolls are real data: one POP909 excerpt\n471\tand four generated variations (cropped to the occupied pitch range for display).\n472\tTable 2: (a) Conditioned on a real window’s own embeddings, the flow reproduces it nearly pixelperfectly with no post-hoc alignment: the PCA-reduced conditioning retains enough to rebuild the\n473\tinput. (b) Note density restored in the masked region under the dropout pipeline of Fig. 4: the full\n474\tinput is always encoded, and conditioning patches under the mask are dropped at the stated per-level\n475\tstrengths. Restoration falls monotonically with dropout strength; spreads are ±1–23%. Both panels:\n476\t10 Euler steps, guidance 1.0, over 6 songs × 3 seeds, with 3 masks in (b).\n477\t0.996 ± 0.004\n478\t\n479\t(a) Reconstruction, pixel F1\n480\t\n481\t(b) Note density restored in masked region vs. dropout strength\n482\tno dropout (reconstruction)\n483\t100%\n484\tfine levels (L4–L5) @ 0.85\n485\t96%\n486\tfine levels (L4–L5) @ 1.0\n487\t76%\n488\tall levels @ 1.0\n489\t53%\n490\t\n491\tFigure 4 shows four inpainted variations of a real excerpt, and Table 2 quantifies both generation and\n492\tinpainting. Infilled notes generally fit the surrounding music, though they follow it less closely than\n493\tlarger diffusion models [16], and some land off. That manifests as variability in how much material\n494\tthe model commits inside the mask. The paradigm absorbs it: an AI Rick Rubin “barely” plays the\n495\tinstrument, and a suggestion need only convey an idea the user implements themselves.\n496\t\n497\t5\n498\t\n499\tOutro: What To Do With This?\n500\t\n501\tWe built a live demo2 that serves not only as an interactive probe of the model’s representational\n502\tcapacity, but as a super fun app that shows how this kind of control feels in practice. Encoding a\n503\t128 × 128 window takes 8.6 ms on two CPU threads, faster than Apple’s Metal backend (19.9 ms). A\n504\tsuggestion (10 Euler steps at guidance 1.0, one function evaluation per step) takes 3.8 s on two CPU\n505\tthreads and 2.8 s on a full CPU (M1 Max, no MPS), within our “timely” design target; MPS drops\n506\tthis to 0.6 s, a laptop RTX 4090 to 0.10 s. Sampling is over 99% of these times, so latency scales\n507\twith function evaluations. Because the model is likelier to under-fill a mask than over-fill it, the demo\n508\tstreams several variations ordered by how many new notes each contains, so the next is ready while\n509\tthe user auditions the last. Future work could extend the temporal field by treating the coarsest levels\n510\tas tokens in a sequence model, or by folding piano-roll images into large squares as in [16]. Adding\n511\tchannels to encode note onsets, velocities, and multiple instruments would lead to richer musical\n512\tcapabilities, and an onset channel would free the separator frames that currently halve the window’s\n513\tspan. While this system is intended for interacting with humans, nothing prevents someone from\n514\twiring it to a production system and closing the loop entirely. However, we strongly believe in AI for\n515\tenhancing humans’ experience of creativity, not for replacing it.\n516\t2 Best experienced firsthand: drscotthawley-midi-rae-jepa-son.hf.space\n517\t\n518\t6\n519\t\n520\t\fAcknowledgments and Disclosure of Funding\n521\tWe thank Razer Corporation and TwinOS for providing the GPU laptops used for some of the\n522\tcomputations in this work.\n523\t\n524\tReferences\n525\t[1] Mahmoud Assran, Quentin Duval, Ishan Misra, Piotr Bojanowski, Pascal Vincent, Michael\n526\tRabbat, Yann LeCun, and Nicolas Ballas. Self-supervised learning from images with a jointembedding predictive architecture. In Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition,\n527\t2023.\n528\t[2] Randall Balestriero and Yann LeCun. LeJEPA: Provable and scalable self-supervised learning\n529\twithout the heuristics, 2025.\n530\t[3] Rachel M. Bittner, Juan José Bosch, David Rubinstein, Gabriel Meseguer-Brocal, and Sebastian\n531\tEwert. A lightweight instrument-agnostic model for polyphonic note transcription and multipitch\n532\testimation. In Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech, and\n533\tSignal Processing (ICASSP), Singapore, 2022.\n534\t[4] Dmitry Bogdanov, Nicolas Wack, Emilia Gómez, Sankalp Gulati, Perfecto Herrera, Oscar\n535\tMayor, Gerard Roma, Justin Salamon, José R. Zapata, and Xavier Serra. Essentia: An audio\n536\tanalysis library for music information retrieval. In Proceedings of the 14th International Society\n537\tfor Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), pages 493–498, Curitiba, Brazil, 2013.\n538\t[5] Mathilde Caron, Hugo Touvron, Ishan Misra, Hervé Jégou, Julien Mairal, Piotr Bojanowski, and\n539\tArmand Joulin. Emerging properties in self-supervised vision transformers. In International\n540\tConference on Computer Vision, 2021.\n541\t[6] Rodrigo Castellon, Chris Donahue, and Percy Liang. Codified audio language modeling learns\n542\tuseful representations for music information retrieval. In Proceedings of the International\n543\tSociety for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), 2021.\n544\t[7] Ke Chen, Gus Xia, and Shlomo Dubnov. Music SketchNet: Controllable music generation via\n545\tfactorized representations of pitch and rhythm. arXiv preprint arXiv:2008.01291, 2020.\n546\t[8] Shoufa Chen, Chongjian Ge, Shilong Zhang, Peize Sun, and Ping Luo. Pixelflow: Pixel-space\n547\tgenerative models with flow, 2025.\n548\t[9] Shuqi Dai, Huan Zhang, and Roger B. Dannenberg. Automatic analysis and influence of\n549\thierarchical structure on melody, rhythm and harmony in popular music. In Proceedings\n550\tof the Joint Conference on AI Music Creativity (AIMC), 2020. arXiv:2010.07518. Humanverified phrase-level structure annotations for POP909 at https://github.com/Dsqvival/\n551\thierarchical-structure-analysis.\n552\t[10] Prafulla Dhariwal and Alex Nichol. Diffusion models beat GANs on image synthesis. In\n553\tAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 2021.\n554\t[11] Sander Dieleman. Generative modelling in latent space, 2025.\n555\t[12] Zach Evans, CJ Carr, Josiah Taylor, Scott H. Hawley, and Jordi Pons. Fast timing-conditioned\n556\tlatent audio diffusion. In Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning\n557\t(ICML), 2024.\n558\t[13] Quentin Garrido, Mahmoud Assran, Nicolas Ballas, Adrien Bardes, Laurent Najman, and Yann\n559\tLeCun. Learning and leveraging world models in visual representation learning. arXiv preprint\n560\tarXiv:2403.00504, 2024.\n561\t[14] David Ha and Jürgen Schmidhuber. World models. arXiv preprint arXiv:1803.10122, 2018.\n562\t[15] Rafik Hachana and Bader Rasheed. Using a joint-embedding predictive architecture for symbolic\n563\tmusic understanding. In NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on AI for Music, 2025.\n564\t7\n565\t\n566\t\f[16] Scott H. Hawley. Pictures of midi: Controlled music generation via graphical prompts for\n567\timage-based diffusion inpainting, 2024.\n568\t[17] Scott H. Hawley. MIDI-RAE-JEPA: Hierarchical representation learning and generation for\n569\tsymbolic music, 2026.\n570\t[18] Hsiao-Tzu Hung, Joann Ching, Seungheon Doh, Nabin Kim, Juhan Nam, and Yi-Hsuan Yang.\n571\tEMOPIA: A multi-modal pop piano dataset for emotion recognition and emotion-based music\n572\tgeneration. In Proc. Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval Conf., 2021.\n573\t[19] Deepak Kumar, Emmanouil Karystinaios, Gerhard Widmer, and Markus Schedl. How far can\n574\tpretrained LLMs go in symbolic music? controlled comparisons of supervised and preferencebased adaptation. arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.22764, 2026.\n575\t[20] Yann LeCun. A path towards autonomous machine intelligence. OpenReview preprint, 2022.\n576\tVersion 0.9.2.\n577\t[21] Yaron Lipman, Ricky T. Q. Chen, Heli Ben-Hamu, Maximilian Nickel, and Matthew Le. Flow\n578\tmatching for generative modeling. In The Eleventh International Conference on Learning\n579\tRepresentations, 2023.\n580\t[22] Ze Liu, Han Hu, Yutong Lin, Zhuliang Yao, Zhenda Xie, Yixuan Wei, Jia Ning, Yue Cao, Zheng\n581\tZhang, Li Dong, et al. Swin transformer V2: Scaling up capacity and resolution. In Conference\n582\ton Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition, 2022.\n583\t[23] Ze Liu, Yutong Lin, Yue Cao, Han Hu, Yixuan Wei, Zheng Zhang, Stephen Lin, and Baining\n584\tGuo. Swin transformer: Hierarchical vision transformer using shifted windows. In International\n585\tConference on Computer Vision, 2021.\n586\t[24] Yiyang Lu, Susie Lu, Qiao Sun, Hanhong Zhao, Zhicheng Jiang, Xianbang Wang, Tianhong\n587\tLi, Zhengyang Geng, and Kaiming He. One-step latent-free image generation with pixel mean\n588\tflows, 2026.\n589\t[25] Yinghao Ma, Siyou Li, Juntao Yu, Emmanouil Benetos, and Akira Maezawa. CMI-Bench: A\n590\tcomprehensive benchmark for evaluating music instruction following. In Proceedings of the\n591\t26th International Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), 2025.\n592\t[26] Ségolène Martin, Anne Gagneux, Paul Hagemann, and Gabriele Steidl. Pnp-flow: Plug-and-play\n593\timage restoration with flow matching. In Y. Yue, A. Garg, N. Peng, F. Sha, and R. Yu, editors,\n594\tInternational Conference on Representation Learning, volume 2025, pages 45466–45492, 2025.\n595\t[27] Brian McFee, Colin Raffel, Dawen Liang, Daniel P. W. Ellis, Matt McVicar, Eric Battenberg,\n596\tand Oriol Nieto. librosa: Audio and music signal analysis in Python. In Proceedings of the 14th\n597\tPython in Science Conference (SciPy), pages 18–24, 2015.\n598\t[28] Lejun Min, Junyan Jiang, Gus Xia, and Jingwei Zhao. Polyffusion: A diffusion model for\n599\tpolyphonic score generation with internal and external controls. In Proceedings of the 24th\n600\tInternational Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), 2023.\n601\t[29] Maxime Oquab, Timothée Darcet, Théo Moutakanni, Huy Vo, Marc Szafraniec, Vasil Khalidov,\n602\tPierre Fernandez, Daniel Haziza, Francisco Massa, Alaaeldin El-Nouby, et al. DINOv2:\n603\tLearning robust visual features without supervision. arXiv preprint arXiv:2304.07193, 2024.\n604\t[30] Ashwini Pokle, Matthew J. Muckley, Ricky T. Q. Chen, and Brian Karrer. Training-free linear\n605\timage inverses via flows. Transactions on Machine Learning Research, 2024.\n606\t[31] Colin Raffel. Learning-Based Methods for Comparing Sequences, with Applications to Audioto-MIDI Alignment and Matching. PhD thesis, Columbia University, 2016.\n607\t[32] Alain Riou, Stefan Lattner, Gaëtan Hadjeres, Michael Anslow, and Geoffroy Peeters. StemJEPA: A joint-embedding predictive architecture for musical stem compatibility estimation. In\n608\tInternational Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference, 2024.\n609\t[33] Simon Rouard and Gaëtan Hadjeres. Crash: Raw audio score-based generative modeling for\n610\tcontrollable high-resolution drum sound synthesis, 2021.\n611\t8\n612\t\n613\t\f[34] Simon Rouard, Michael Krause, Axel Roebel, Carl-Johann Simon-Gabriel, and Alexandre\n614\tDéfossez. Muscriptor: An open model for multi-instrument music transcription, 2026.\n615\t[35] Rick Rubin. Rick Rubin: The 60 minutes interview. 60 Minutes, CBS News. Interview by\n616\tAnderson Cooper, January 2023.\n617\t[36] Chih-Pin Tan, Alvin W. Y. Su, and Yi-Hsuan Yang. Melody infilling with user-provided\n618\tstructural context. In Proceedings of the 23rd International Society for Music Information\n619\tRetrieval Conference (ISMIR), Bengaluru, India, 2022.\n620\t[37] Alexander Tong, Kilian FATRAS, Nikolay Malkin, Guillaume Huguet, Yanlei Zhang, Jarrid\n621\tRector-Brooks, Guy Wolf, and Yoshua Bengio. Improving and generalizing flow-based generative models with minibatch optimal transport. Transactions on Machine Learning Research,\n622\t2024. Expert Certification.\n623\t[38] Ziyu Wang, Kun Fang, and Yann LeCun. Music-JEPA: Learning a world model of sound from\n624\taction, 2026.\n625\t[39] Haiyu Wu, Randall Balestriero, and Morgan Levine. Visreg: Variance-invariance-sketching\n626\tregularization for jepa training, 2026.\n627\t[40] Xiao Wu, Zihao Huang, Kai Zhang, Jun Yu, Xu Tan, Tao Zhang, Yan Li, Zhan Wang, and\n628\tLingling Sun. MelodyGLM: Multi-task pre-training for symbolic melody generation. arXiv\n629\tpreprint arXiv:2309.10738, 2023.\n630\t[41] Meng Yang, Jon McCormack, Maria Teresa Llano, Wanchao Su, and Chao Lei. MIDI-LLaMA:\n631\tAn instruction-following multimodal LLM for symbolic music understanding. arXiv preprint\n632\tarXiv:2601.21740, 2026.\n633\t[42] Mingyang Yao and Zhaoxiang Feng. ARIMA: Reconstruction-grounded predictive representation learning for symbolic music. arXiv preprint arXiv:2607.10003, 2026.\n634\t[43] Jiahao Zhao, Yunjia Li, Wei Li, and Kazuyoshi Yoshii. ABC-Eval: Benchmarking large\n635\tlanguage models on symbolic music understanding and instruction following. arXiv preprint\n636\tarXiv:2509.23350, 2025.\n637\t[44] Bowen Zheng, Nan Ma, Shengbang Tong, and Saining Xie. Diffusion transformers with\n638\trepresentation autoencoders. arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.11690, 2025.\n639\t\n640\t9\n641\t\n642\t\fSupplemental Materials / Appendices\n643\tThe following appendices archive the complete measurement record behind the paper. An interactive\n644\tversion of these tables, with listening examples and the live demo, is maintained at drscotthawley.\n645\tgithub.io/midi-rae-jepa-son.\n646\t\n647\tA\n648\t\n649\tEncoder training objective: equations and details\n650\t\n651\tThe total training objective combines four terms:\n652\tL = λLequiv + (1 − λ)LSIGReg + λMEP LMEP + λfact Lfact\n653\t\n654\t(1)\n655\t\n656\tapplied level-wise: Lequiv and LSIGReg act only at L0–L3 (at L4 and L5, SIGReg washes out the fine\n657\tnote-level structure those levels carry, and patch-level equivariance is less meaningful at those scales),\n658\tand Lfact , described at the end of this appendix, acts only at the three coarsest levels L0–L2.\n659\tView generation. Two views are created by applying random shifts in time and pitch: x1 is the\n660\toriginal crop and x2 is shifted by (∆x , ∆y ) pixels. Both views are full 128 × 128 windows extracted\n661\tfrom the song’s complete piano roll; a “shift” is implemented by sampling the second window at a\n662\tposition offset by (∆x , ∆y ), so views always retain the encoder’s input size.\n663\tEquivariance loss. A naive attraction loss collapses all shifted pairs to identical embeddings\n664\tregardless of shift magnitude. Instead we enforce a target embedding distance proportional to the\n665\tshift magnitude, so that larger shifts produce proportionally more distant embeddings:\n666\t\u0010\n667\t\u00112\n668\t√\n669\t(2)\n670\tLequiv (z1 , z2 , δ) = ∥z1 − z2 ∥ − α d ∥δ̂∥\n671\twhere z1 is the teacher embedding of x1 and z2 is the student embedding of x2 (see the EMA teacher\n672\tparagraph below), ∥ · ∥ denotes the Euclidean (L2) norm, d is the embedding dimension, δ̂ is the shift\n673\tδ = (∆x , ∆y ) with each component divided by its per-axis maximum\n674\t(∆tmax , ∆pmax ) so that each\n675\t√\n676\tcomponent lies in [0, 1], and α is a scalar hyperparameter. The d factor accounts for concentration\n677\tof measure in high-dimensional spaces, giving α consistent semantic meaning across all hierarchy\n678\tlevels. Unlike a hinge loss, this smooth quadratic both attracts pairs that are too far apart and repels\n679\tpairs that are too close. Shifts are sampled from a Beta(2, 2) distribution up to (∆tmax , ∆pmax )\n680\tpixels, with ∆pmax = 12 (one octave) and ∆tmax = 48 for the models in this paper.\n681\tChunked SIGReg. SIGReg uses the Epps–Pulley characteristic-function test to enforce an isotropic\n682\tGaussian prior on embeddings [2]. SIGReg computes a tensor of shape (N, Nslices , T ) with Nslices =\n683\t256; on 16 GB GPUs this tensor alone consumed ∼5 GB. We chunk the slice dimension into groups\n684\tof 32 and accumulate in float32:\n685\tP (k)\n686\tLSIGReg ≈ k LSIGReg\n687\t(3)\n688\twhere k indexes the chunks. This reduces peak VRAM by ∼5 GB and counterintuitively improves\n689\tthroughput by reducing memory-allocator pressure. SIGReg is applied only to student embeddings\n690\tz2 .\n691\tEMA teacher and masked embedding prediction. Following DINO [5] and I-JEPA [1], we\n692\tmaintain an EMA teacher θT ← η θT + (1 − η) θS with η = 0.96, initialised from the student. The\n693\tteacher produces stable target embeddings z1 for the equivariance loss and processes x2 without\n694\tmasking to supply prediction targets for the MEP; a lightweight predictor, conditioned on the student’s\n695\tfull-context representation, predicts the teacher embeddings at randomly masked patch positions. The\n696\tteacher is not redundant with SIGReg: SIGReg alone prevents collapse; the teacher exists to provide\n697\tstable, slowly-varying regression targets. The MEP loss is averaged over hierarchy levels:\n698\tL\n699\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "aec23075-6a45-40bf-a634-30b46d0a002f", "uuid": "2e5eec7f-d8ce-4eb3-befa-02d4c7a60613", "timestamp": "2026-08-06T07:31:40.838Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2608.04378.txt", "content": "compared to smaller, targeted models [25], a finding that persists in the symbolic domain [43, 19].\nSystems that close the gap do so by attaching domain-specific perception modules to the LLM [41],\nthe same role the “ears” play in our system.\nGenerative music models implicitly encode musical structure and style [6, 12, 28], but implicit\nencoding is not articulation: a co-writing partner must say what structures are present and what\nchanges might improve a composition or arrangement. Machine agents could exchange feedback as\nmathematical signals, e.g. as gradients toward some endpoint, as in classifier guidance [10], but a\nhuman collaborator cannot act on a gradient. Description and reproduction make different demands:\nrepresentation learning and reconstruction are separate tasks, long regarded as a tradeoff [11].\nRepresentation autoencoders (RAEs) [44] show the tradeoff is an artifact of compression: their latents\nrearrange the input at nearly its original dimensionality, with reconstruction handled separately rather\nthan by the representation objective. The pairing supports discriminative tasks and high-quality\ngeneration alike. We adopt this paradigm for music, with a conditional flow model standing in for the\ntrained decoder: a single encoder, trained with no reconstruction objective and then frozen, serving\nas the ears and conditioning the mouth.\nThe internal representations are formed by a joint-embedding predictive architecture (JEPA) [20, 1].\nJEPA has been applied to music before: Stem-JEPA [32] predicted musical stem compatibility\nfrom audio; Hachana and Rasheed [15] adapted JEPA to tokenized symbolic music with musically\nmotivated masking of instruments, pitch classes and octaves, and found the learned representation\ndominated by positional information, which limited transfer to content tasks such as genre and style;\nand, concurrently with this work, Music-JEPA [38] learned an action-conditioned audio world model\nfrom paired piano audio and performance data, while ARIMA [42] learned windowed latent-predictive\nrepresentations of symbolic music. Unlike these, our approach operates purely on symbolic (MIDI)\npiano-roll images, is trained with a hierarchical, multi-level Swin V2 encoder rather than a single-scale\nbackbone, prevents representational collapse via a LeJEPA-style distributional regularizer (SIGReg)\nrather than an EMA teacher, and makes shift structure explicit in the objective rather than leaving it\nto be absorbed by positional encodings. This paper extends our earlier report [17], which details the\narchitecture and training objectives; here we focus on the musical and co-creative capabilities, and\nreport improvements to the model and the generative pipeline together with an assessment of gains\nfrom supervised signals and additional datasets.\nTo maximize accessibility for GPU-poor musicians (who may also prefer not to send works-inprogress to a cloud service) we adopted a fundamental engineering constraint: timely inference on\nCPU-only runtimes, demonstrated by a live demo linked from the upplemental materials.1\n1 Supplemental Materials: drscotthawley.github.io/midi-rae-jepa-son\n\n2\n\n\fFigure 2: Just as a vision world model pans and tilts a camera to scan a panoramic scene, we take\nsquare crops of MIDI piano-roll images and compare crops translated in time by ∆x time steps\nor in pitch by ∆y semitones. Given one crop and a shift vector (∆x, ∆y), the model predicts\nthe embedding of the shifted crop. Regions outside the image are filled with blanks. Our design\nrequirement of fast CPU execution fixes the crop at 128×128 pixels. Pixels are binary, so note\nvelocity is discarded, which is acceptable for the songwriting use case. Time is quantized to 32nd\nnotes on a tempo-normalized grid, the finest grid at which a blank frame can separate two repeated\nnotes in a binary image, so a crop spans four bars in 4/4, or eight seconds at 120 BPM.\nL5\n\nL4\n\nL3\n\nL2\n\nL1\n\nL0\n\n4×4 patches\n\n8×8 patches\n\n16×16 patches\n\n32×32 patches\n\n64×64 patches\n\n128×128 patch\n\n(a)\n\n(b)\n\n2× ↓\n\n32×32\n\ndepth=2\ndim=8\nheads=2\n\n2× ↓\n\n2× ↓\n\ndepth=2\n\ndepth=2\ndim=32\nheads=2\n\n16×16 dim=16\n\n8×8\n\n2× ↓\n\ndepth=6\n\n4×4 dim=64\nheads=4\n\n2× ↓\n\ndepth=2\n\n2×2 dim=128\nheads=8\n\ndepth=2\n\n1×1 dim=256\nheads=16\n\nheads=2\n\nFigure 3: Our 2.55 million-parameter hierarchical Swin V2 Encoder. Model architecture hyperparameters were selected via ablation studies detailed in the Supplemental Materials (Appendices A–F).\n\n2\n\nHow: A Symbolic Music “World Model”\n“It wasn’t made by people who went to the music conservatory. It was made by kids who felt\nsomething.”\n—Rick Rubin, on early hip-hop [35]\n\nRather than relying on supervised labels and the vocabulary of music theory, we train a self-supervised\nmodel that builds its own (hierarchical) representations of music, its own ‘feel’ for musical structure.\nIt does this by exploring the “space” of musical compositions, cf. Figure 2. Human musical\nrepresentations are hierarchical: notes, chords, phrases, song structure. Yet labeled data for that\nhierarchy is limited. Chord detectors and song-structure predictors can supply some of it, but we\nwant to learn the hierarchy without labels, the way World Models in computer vision [14, 13] build\nsemantic representations by predicting embeddings of one view of a scene from another. We do this\non piano-roll images, partly to take advantage of the Swin V2 transformer [23, 22], a hierarchical\nrepresentation learner already proven on images, and partly because a piano roll is a semantic proxy\nfor an audio spectrogram, with perceptually aligned axes. Latents trained for reconstruction accuracy\nrarely become semantically meaningful; they tend instead to resemble spatially downsampled copies\nof the input. Following the RAE paradigm, ours is a latent model with no compression: the latents hold\nnearly as many numbers as the input (16,384 pixels → 16,128 floats), rearranged into a meaningful\ngeometry rather than a smaller one.\nTraining objective. The encoder is trained with a combined self-supervised objective,\nL = λLequiv + (1 − λ)LSIGReg + λMEP LMEP + λfact Lfact ,\napplied level-wise. Lequiv is an equivariance loss that pulls together or pushes apart embeddings\nof shifted crop pairs in proportion to the shift magnitude, so the latent space respects pitch/time\ntranslation structure rather than collapsing to shift-invariance. LSIGReg is LeJEPA’s sketched isotropic\nGaussian regularization [2], which applies an Epps–Pulley test along random projections to enforce\nan isotropic Gaussian prior, preventing representational collapse. (We also tried VISReg [39], which\n3\n\n\freplaces the Epps–Pulley test with a sliced-Wasserstein distance, and saw no consistent improvement\nin our metrics.) The equivariance and SIGReg losses are applied only at L0–L3: at L4 and L5,\nSIGReg washes out the fine note-level structure those levels carry, and patch-level equivariance is less\nmeaningful at those scales. LMEP is an intra- and inter-level masked-embedding-prediction loss in the\nstyle of I-JEPA [1]: an auxiliary predictor infers an EMA teacher’s embeddings from the student’s full\ncontext. Lfact is a soft factorization loss which pulls pitch- and time-shift difference vectors toward\nparallel, anti-parallel, or orthogonal geometry depending on augmentation type. It is applied only at\nL0–L2, enforcing equivariance out to larger shifts than Lequiv achieves alone [17]. Full derivations\nand hyperparameters are given in the Supplemental Materials (Appendices A–F).\n\n3\n\nEars: What Does It Actually “Perceive”?\n\nThe “ears” of a full system such as that diagrammed in Figure 1 can partially consist of libraries\nof lightweight audio feature detectors, predicting categories such as genre, instrument type, and\nmood [4], or metrics such as pitch and tempo [27]. In addition, independent detectors for chords,\nkeys, phrase boundaries and song structure are available. For songwriting, we are concerned less with\nnuances of performance than with the notes the player intended. Discarding audio detail in favor of a\nstructured MIDI representation therefore costs us little. Audio-to-MIDI conversion [3] recovers that\nintent in a compact, semantic form already familiar from many music creators’ workflows, and recent\nimprovements [34] have made it reliable enough to build on. We treat it as upstream of this work.\nOur encoder is not meant to replace those small detectors. It learns representations that resist easy\nlabeling, its own sense of “feel,” obtained only by exploring the space of musical compositions. To\ngauge what those representations correspond to musically, we turn to standard probes. Each probe\nbelow trains a small linear (or ridge) model on frozen, per-level embeddings, or measures a geometric\nproperty of the embedding space directly; none fine-tune the encoder.\nWe call the model introduced here MIDI-RAE-JEPA-SON (MRJS), a successor of our earlier\nreport’s MIDI-RAE-JEPA [17], which appears in Table 1 as MRJ-48∧ (∆tmax =48, factorization\nloss active), alongside a DINOv2 [29] baseline and a chance column ( ). Unlike MRJ-48∧, whose\npipeline components were trained against differing train/test splits on a dataset since found to contain\ncorruption, MRJS and every downstream component share a single unified split on corrected data.\nThat column is therefore historical reference, not a controlled comparison. The table reports each\nprobe at the hierarchy level where it scores best, and those levels carry as much information as the\nscores. Phrase boundaries peak at the coarse end (L0–L2) while note density and harmonic content\npeak at the fine end (L4–L5), so the level at which a property becomes decodable tracks the musical\nTable 1: Probing frozen embeddings for musical structure. Each cell reports a model’s best value\nacross its hierarchy levels (minimum for ↓ metrics), with the winning level in small type and a bar\nwhose height is proportional to that level’s receptive-field size (L0 tallest); = chance level for that\nmetric; bold = best model per row; cells are shaded per row with the plasma colormap (yellow =\nbest). Cross-song: whether same-song embeddings sit closer together than cross-song ones. Time\nR2 : how well the distance between two embeddings predicts the temporal offset between their crops.\nJoint Chord: root and chord quality jointly. Chroma R2 : recovery of the local 12-bin pitch-class\ndistribution. Phrase AP/AUC: linear probe detecting whether a human-annotated phrase boundary\nfalls within half a bar of a crop’s center, scored as average precision and ROC-AUC.\nMetric\n\nDINOv2 MRJ-48∧ MRJS +chords +phrases Lakh-1× Lakh-4×\n2\n\nNote Density R ↑\nCross-song ↓\nTime R2 ↑\nRoot Note ↑\nJoint Chord ↑\nKey Detection ↑\nChroma R2 ↑\nPhrase AP ↑\nPhrase AUC ↑\n\nn/a\nn/a\nn/a\n.08\n.05\n.05\n.00\n.17\n.50\n\n.93 L1\n.68 L1\n.23 L1\n.24 L0\n.17 L1\n.19 L0\n.36 L1\n.21 L0\n.57 L0\n\n.93 L5\n.69 L5\n.24 L2\n.25 L5\n.17 L5\n.18 L5\n.54 L4\n.28 L1\n.61 L2\n\n.88 L5\n.80 L3\n.26 L4\n.24 L5\n.18 L5\n.16 L5\n.65 L5\n.27 L0\n.61 L0\n\n4\n\n.91 L5\n.73 L1\n.24 L1\n.59 L2\n.54 L3\n.70 L3\n.83 L4\n.25 L2\n.58 L0\n\n.89 L5\n.57 L3\n.24 L2\n.22 L5\n.18 L5\n.17 L5\n.75 L5\n.29 L2\n.62 L0\n\n.94 L5\n.62 L4\n.25 L2\n.22 L5\n.18 L5\n.15 L5\n.76 L5\n.28 L1\n.61 L1\n\n.94 L5\n.77 L5\n.22 L3\n.26 L5\n.20 L5\n.18 L5\n.61 L5\n.26 L0\n.61 L0\n\n\ftime scale on which it operates. Temporal offset is the weakest probe throughout, .22–.26 for every\nmodel, which is expected: a piano roll is nearly stationary along the time axis, so sliding a crop\nsideways yields more of the same, whereas pitch is absolute and a vertical shift moves material into a\ndifferent register. We read the harmony scores as capacity probes, not as downstream performance;\nsmall dedicated chord detectors do that job far better. The DINOv2 baseline is instructive: on\nproperties that reduce to image statistics it is competitive or better, reaching .93 on note density\nand separating songs more sharply than MRJS, but it falls away on the musically specific probes,\nrecovering chroma at .36 against .65 and phrase boundaries at .21 AP against .27.\nTo assess the effects of dataset variation and size, we trained encoders on subsets of the Lakh MIDI\nDataset [31] containing 1× and 4× as many songs as POP909. Both transfer well: on the POP909\nprobes they match or exceed MRJS on note density and cross-song separation. At matched training\nbudget, however, the 4× subset gave no consistent gain over 1×, and degraded chroma (.76 to .61)\nand cross-song separation (.62 to .77). We also explored adding auxiliary supervised objectives to the\nself-supervised recipe: chord recognition, using POP909’s chord annotations, and phrase-boundary\nprediction, using the human-verified annotations of Dai et al. [9], each applied as a small linear head\non the coarse levels during training. Chord supervision makes harmonic content linearly decodable\nwhere it was barely present: root identification rises from .24 to .59, joint chord from .18 to .54, and\nkey detection, which was never supervised, from .16 to .70. It also moves the seat of that information\nfrom L5 (near note-level) to the coarser and more “abstract” L2–L3. The cost is small: phrase AP\nfalls from .27 to .25 and temporal offset from .26 to .24, while note density and cross-song separation\nboth improve. Phrase supervision helps less, but is not without effect: boundary detection rises only\nslightly (AP .27 to .29, AUC .61 to .62), while cross-song separation improves markedly (.80 to .57).\nHarmonic content, then, has to be asked for; temporal and phrase structure largely emerge from the\nself-supervised objectives alone. Once the inputs are encoded, they can be sent to the “brain” as well\nas used as conditioning signals for the mouth described next.\n\n4\n\nMouth: Making Suggestions\n\nVerbal feedback and lyrical suggestions can be handled by a properly harnessed LLM. Musical\nsuggestions benefit from their own generative model conditioned on the idea the user already has,\nas in an inpainting task where we replace a melody or an accompaniment, or suggest a different\ncontinuation. Hachana and Rasheed [15] anticipate that a reliable JEPA music representation could\nenable generative applications; this section realizes one. Autoregressive transformers generate left\nto right, so filling a hole in the middle of an existing passage is not something they do natively, and\nmust be trained for specifically [36]. Diffusion models over piano-roll images, by contrast, support\ninpainting at inference time essentially for free [28, 16], and in a form that admits graphical prompts:\nthe user draws on the piano roll where they want new notes, and the model generates notes suiting the\nsurrounding material. But the price of this power is inference cost. Diffusion inpainting needs many\nintegration steps, and our own earlier system, Pictures of MIDI [16], was large and slow enough\nto require a GPU. That is disqualifying under our design constraint. We therefore moved to flow\nmatching [21], which can generate in very few integration steps: the smooth flow admits higher-order\nintegration schemes such as RK4, and optimal-transport pairing [37] during training straightens the\ntrajectories, making even Euler steps sufficient.\nRecent work has favored flowing in pixel space rather than in the latent space [8, 24]. We do the same:\nwe flow from noise in pixel space, conditioned on embeddings of the user’s existing musical idea. We\nsaid earlier that there is no compression in the embeddings; however, we find it sufficient to reduce the\nconditioning signal via PCA, keeping at least 90% of the variance per level, resulting in a reduction\nof over 3× (16,128 → 4,835 floats). This still reconstructs the input closely (F1=0.996), yet the goal\nis variations rather than copies. How far those variations stray is controllable: guidance strength sets\nhow tightly a sample follows its conditioning, and dropping conditioning at different levels loosens it\nfurther. That dropout also points to how we handle inpainting. To inpaint, we apply spatial dropout\nto the PCA conditioning at the locations the user painted. The flow was trained with exactly this\ndropout, so it fills the gap from surrounding context without needing an inpainting-specific sampler,\nunlike sampling-time schemes for diffusion models [33], and we adopt none of the flow-specific\nschemes [30, 26]. Varying the dropout probability per level sets how coarse or fine the replacement\nis: dropping only the fine levels rewrites notes while keeping the harmonic frame, dropping every\nlevel rewrites the passage.\n5\n\n\fEncoder\n\nPCA\n\nDropout\n\nInput\nFlow\nHand-drawn mask\n\nNoise\n\nVariations - 4 seeds\n(actual data)\n\nFigure 4: Unified generation and inpainting. The encoder’s PCA-reduced conditioning maps pass\nthrough a dropout stage that zeroes patches under a hand-drawn mask, at user-chosen strengths per\nlevel; a flow-matching model then generates a window from noise, guided only by the surviving\nconditioning (10 Euler steps, guidance 1). Outside the mask the output is overwritten with the input,\npreserving untouched music exactly. With no mask, the same pipeline performs ordinary conditioned\ngeneration, varied by seed or guidance strength. The piano rolls are real data: one POP909 excerpt\nand four generated variations (cropped to the occupied pitch range for display).\nTable 2: (a) Conditioned on a real window’s own embeddings, the flow reproduces it nearly pixelperfectly with no post-hoc alignment: the PCA-reduced conditioning retains enough to rebuild the\ninput. (b) Note density restored in the masked region under the dropout pipeline of Fig. 4: the full\ninput is always encoded, and conditioning patches under the mask are dropped at the stated per-level\nstrengths. Restoration falls monotonically with dropout strength; spreads are ±1–23%. Both panels:\n10 Euler steps, guidance 1.0, over 6 songs × 3 seeds, with 3 masks in (b).\n0.996 ± 0.004\n\n(a) Reconstruction, pixel F1\n\n(b) Note density restored in masked region vs. dropout strength\nno dropout (reconstruction)\n100%\nfine levels (L4–L5) @ 0.85\n96%\nfine levels (L4–L5) @ 1.0\n76%\nall levels @ 1.0\n53%\n\nFigure 4 shows four inpainted variations of a real excerpt, and Table 2 quantifies both generation and\ninpainting. Infilled notes generally fit the surrounding music, though they follow it less closely than\nlarger diffusion models [16], and some land off. That manifests as variability in how much material\nthe model commits inside the mask. The paradigm absorbs it: an AI Rick Rubin “barely” plays the\ninstrument, and a suggestion need only convey an idea the user implements themselves.\n\n5\n\nOutro: What To Do With This?\n\nWe built a live demo2 that serves not only as an interactive probe of the model’s representational\ncapacity, but as a super fun app that shows how this kind of control feels in practice. Encoding a\n128 × 128 window takes 8.6 ms on two CPU threads, faster than Apple’s Metal backend (19.9 ms). A\nsuggestion (10 Euler steps at guidance 1.0, one function evaluation per step) takes 3.8 s on two CPU\nthreads and 2.8 s on a full CPU (M1 Max, no MPS), within our “timely” design target; MPS drops\nthis to 0.6 s, a laptop RTX 4090 to 0.10 s. Sampling is over 99% of these times, so latency scales\nwith function evaluations. Because the model is likelier to under-fill a mask than over-fill it, the demo\nstreams several variations ordered by how many new notes each contains, so the next is ready while\nthe user auditions the last. Future work could extend the temporal field by treating the coarsest levels\nas tokens in a sequence model, or by folding piano-roll images into large squares as in [16]. Adding\nchannels to encode note onsets, velocities, and multiple instruments would lead to richer musical\ncapabilities, and an onset channel would free the separator frames that currently halve the window’s\nspan. While this system is intended for interacting with humans, nothing prevents someone from\nwiring it to a production system and closing the loop entirely. However, we strongly believe in AI for\nenhancing humans’ experience of creativity, not for replacing it.\n2 Best experienced firsthand: drscotthawley-midi-rae-jepa-son.hf.space\n\n6\n\n\fAcknowledgments and Disclosure of Funding\nWe thank Razer Corporation and TwinOS for providing the GPU laptops used for some of the\ncomputations in this work.\n\nReferences\n[1] Mahmoud Assran, Quentin Duval, Ishan Misra, Piotr Bojanowski, Pascal Vincent, Michael\nRabbat, Yann LeCun, and Nicolas Ballas. Self-supervised learning from images with a jointembedding predictive architecture. In Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition,\n2023.\n[2] Randall Balestriero and Yann LeCun. LeJEPA: Provable and scalable self-supervised learning\nwithout the heuristics, 2025.\n[3] Rachel M. Bittner, Juan José Bosch, David Rubinstein, Gabriel Meseguer-Brocal, and Sebastian\nEwert. A lightweight instrument-agnostic model for polyphonic note transcription and multipitch\nestimation. In Proceedings of the IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech, and\nSignal Processing (ICASSP), Singapore, 2022.\n[4] Dmitry Bogdanov, Nicolas Wack, Emilia Gómez, Sankalp Gulati, Perfecto Herrera, Oscar\nMayor, Gerard Roma, Justin Salamon, José R. Zapata, and Xavier Serra. Essentia: An audio\nanalysis library for music information retrieval. In Proceedings of the 14th International Society\nfor Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), pages 493–498, Curitiba, Brazil, 2013.\n[5] Mathilde Caron, Hugo Touvron, Ishan Misra, Hervé Jégou, Julien Mairal, Piotr Bojanowski, and\nArmand Joulin. Emerging properties in self-supervised vision transformers. In International\nConference on Computer Vision, 2021.\n[6] Rodrigo Castellon, Chris Donahue, and Percy Liang. Codified audio language modeling learns\nuseful representations for music information retrieval. In Proceedings of the International\nSociety for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), 2021.\n[7] Ke Chen, Gus Xia, and Shlomo Dubnov. Music SketchNet: Controllable music generation via\nfactorized representations of pitch and rhythm. arXiv preprint arXiv:2008.01291, 2020.\n[8] Shoufa Chen, Chongjian Ge, Shilong Zhang, Peize Sun, and Ping Luo. Pixelflow: Pixel-space\ngenerative models with flow, 2025.\n[9] Shuqi Dai, Huan Zhang, and Roger B. Dannenberg. Automatic analysis and influence of\nhierarchical structure on melody, rhythm and harmony in popular music. In Proceedings\nof the Joint Conference on AI Music Creativity (AIMC), 2020. arXiv:2010.07518. Humanverified phrase-level structure annotations for POP909 at https://github.com/Dsqvival/\nhierarchical-structure-analysis.\n[10] Prafulla Dhariwal and Alex Nichol. Diffusion models beat GANs on image synthesis. In\nAdvances in Neural Information Processing Systems, 2021.\n[11] Sander Dieleman. Generative modelling in latent space, 2025.\n[12] Zach Evans, CJ Carr, Josiah Taylor, Scott H. Hawley, and Jordi Pons. Fast timing-conditioned\nlatent audio diffusion. In Proceedings of the International Conference on Machine Learning\n(ICML), 2024.\n[13] Quentin Garrido, Mahmoud Assran, Nicolas Ballas, Adrien Bardes, Laurent Najman, and Yann\nLeCun. Learning and leveraging world models in visual representation learning. arXiv preprint\narXiv:2403.00504, 2024.\n[14] David Ha and Jürgen Schmidhuber. World models. arXiv preprint arXiv:1803.10122, 2018.\n[15] Rafik Hachana and Bader Rasheed. Using a joint-embedding predictive architecture for symbolic\nmusic understanding. In NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on AI for Music, 2025.\n7\n\n\f[16] Scott H. Hawley. Pictures of midi: Controlled music generation via graphical prompts for\nimage-based diffusion inpainting, 2024.\n[17] Scott H. Hawley. MIDI-RAE-JEPA: Hierarchical representation learning and generation for\nsymbolic music, 2026.\n[18] Hsiao-Tzu Hung, Joann Ching, Seungheon Doh, Nabin Kim, Juhan Nam, and Yi-Hsuan Yang.\nEMOPIA: A multi-modal pop piano dataset for emotion recognition and emotion-based music\ngeneration. In Proc. Int. Society for Music Information Retrieval Conf., 2021.\n[19] Deepak Kumar, Emmanouil Karystinaios, Gerhard Widmer, and Markus Schedl. How far can\npretrained LLMs go in symbolic music? controlled comparisons of supervised and preferencebased adaptation. arXiv preprint arXiv:2601.22764, 2026.\n[20] Yann LeCun. A path towards autonomous machine intelligence. OpenReview preprint, 2022.\nVersion 0.9.2.\n[21] Yaron Lipman, Ricky T. Q. Chen, Heli Ben-Hamu, Maximilian Nickel, and Matthew Le. Flow\nmatching for generative modeling. In The Eleventh International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations, 2023.\n[22] Ze Liu, Han Hu, Yutong Lin, Zhuliang Yao, Zhenda Xie, Yixuan Wei, Jia Ning, Yue Cao, Zheng\nZhang, Li Dong, et al. Swin transformer V2: Scaling up capacity and resolution. In Conference\non Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition, 2022.\n[23] Ze Liu, Yutong Lin, Yue Cao, Han Hu, Yixuan Wei, Zheng Zhang, Stephen Lin, and Baining\nGuo. Swin transformer: Hierarchical vision transformer using shifted windows. In International\nConference on Computer Vision, 2021.\n[24] Yiyang Lu, Susie Lu, Qiao Sun, Hanhong Zhao, Zhicheng Jiang, Xianbang Wang, Tianhong\nLi, Zhengyang Geng, and Kaiming He. One-step latent-free image generation with pixel mean\nflows, 2026.\n[25] Yinghao Ma, Siyou Li, Juntao Yu, Emmanouil Benetos, and Akira Maezawa. CMI-Bench: A\ncomprehensive benchmark for evaluating music instruction following. In Proceedings of the\n26th International Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), 2025.\n[26] Ségolène Martin, Anne Gagneux, Paul Hagemann, and Gabriele Steidl. Pnp-flow: Plug-and-play\nimage restoration with flow matching. In Y. Yue, A. Garg, N. Peng, F. Sha, and R. Yu, editors,\nInternational Conference on Representation Learning, volume 2025, pages 45466–45492, 2025.\n[27] Brian McFee, Colin Raffel, Dawen Liang, Daniel P. W. Ellis, Matt McVicar, Eric Battenberg,\nand Oriol Nieto. librosa: Audio and music signal analysis in Python. In Proceedings of the 14th\nPython in Science Conference (SciPy), pages 18–24, 2015.\n[28] Lejun Min, Junyan Jiang, Gus Xia, and Jingwei Zhao. Polyffusion: A diffusion model for\npolyphonic score generation with internal and external controls. In Proceedings of the 24th\nInternational Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference (ISMIR), 2023.\n[29] Maxime Oquab, Timothée Darcet, Théo Moutakanni, Huy Vo, Marc Szafraniec, Vasil Khalidov,\nPierre Fernandez, Daniel Haziza, Francisco Massa, Alaaeldin El-Nouby, et al. DINOv2:\nLearning robust visual features without supervision. arXiv preprint arXiv:2304.07193, 2024.\n[30] Ashwini Pokle, Matthew J. Muckley, Ricky T. Q. Chen, and Brian Karrer. Training-free linear\nimage inverses via flows. Transactions on Machine Learning Research, 2024.\n[31] Colin Raffel. Learning-Based Methods for Comparing Sequences, with Applications to Audioto-MIDI Alignment and Matching. PhD thesis, Columbia University, 2016.\n[32] Alain Riou, Stefan Lattner, Gaëtan Hadjeres, Michael Anslow, and Geoffroy Peeters. StemJEPA: A joint-embedding predictive architecture for musical stem compatibility estimation. In\nInternational Society for Music Information Retrieval Conference, 2024.\n[33] Simon Rouard and Gaëtan Hadjeres. Crash: Raw audio score-based generative modeling for\ncontrollable high-resolution drum sound synthesis, 2021.\n8\n\n\f[34] Simon Rouard, Michael Krause, Axel Roebel, Carl-Johann Simon-Gabriel, and Alexandre\nDéfossez. Muscriptor: An open model for multi-instrument music transcription, 2026.\n[35] Rick Rubin. Rick Rubin: The 60 minutes interview. 60 Minutes, CBS News. Interview by\nAnderson Cooper, January 2023.\n[36] Chih-Pin Tan, Alvin W. Y. Su, and Yi-Hsuan Yang. Melody infilling with user-provided\nstructural context. In Proceedings of the 23rd International Society for Music Information\nRetrieval Conference (ISMIR), Bengaluru, India, 2022.\n[37] Alexander Tong, Kilian FATRAS, Nikolay Malkin, Guillaume Huguet, Yanlei Zhang, Jarrid\nRector-Brooks, Guy Wolf, and Yoshua Bengio. Improving and generalizing flow-based generative models with minibatch optimal transport. Transactions on Machine Learning Research,\n2024. Expert Certification.\n[38] Ziyu Wang, Kun Fang, and Yann LeCun. Music-JEPA: Learning a world model of sound from\naction, 2026.\n[39] Haiyu Wu, Randall Balestriero, and Morgan Levine. Visreg: Variance-invariance-sketching\nregularization for jepa training, 2026.\n[40] Xiao Wu, Zihao Huang, Kai Zhang, Jun Yu, Xu Tan, Tao Zhang, Yan Li, Zhan Wang, and\nLingling Sun. MelodyGLM: Multi-task pre-training for symbolic melody generation. arXiv\npreprint arXiv:2309.10738, 2023.\n[41] Meng Yang, Jon McCormack, Maria Teresa Llano, Wanchao Su, and Chao Lei. MIDI-LLaMA:\nAn instruction-following multimodal LLM for symbolic music understanding. arXiv preprint\narXiv:2601.21740, 2026.\n[42] Mingyang Yao and Zhaoxiang Feng. ARIMA: Reconstruction-grounded predictive representation learning for symbolic music. arXiv preprint arXiv:2607.10003, 2026.\n[43] Jiahao Zhao, Yunjia Li, Wei Li, and Kazuyoshi Yoshii. ABC-Eval: Benchmarking large\nlanguage models on symbolic music understanding and instruction following. arXiv preprint\narXiv:2509.23350, 2025.\n[44] Bowen Zheng, Nan Ma, Shengbang Tong, and Saining Xie. Diffusion transformers with\nrepresentation autoencoders. arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.11690, 2025.\n\n9\n\n\fSupplemental Materials / Appendices\nThe following appendices archive the complete measurement record behind the paper. An interactive\nversion of these tables, with listening examples and the live demo, is maintained at drscotthawley.\ngithub.io/midi-rae-jepa-son.\n\nA\n\nEncoder training objective: equations and details\n\nThe total training objective combines four terms:\nL = λLequiv + (1 − λ)LSIGReg + λMEP LMEP + λfact Lfact\n\n(1)\n\napplied level-wise: Lequiv and LSIGReg act only at L0–L3 (at L4 and L5, SIGReg washes out the fine\nnote-level structure those levels carry, and patch-level equivariance is less meaningful at those scales),\nand Lfact , described at the end of this appendix, acts only at the three coarsest levels L0–L2.\nView generation. Two views are created by applying random shifts in time and pitch: x1 is the\noriginal crop and x2 is shifted by (∆x , ∆y ) pixels. Both views are full 128 × 128 windows extracted\nfrom the song’s complete piano roll; a “shift” is implemented by sampling the second window at a\nposition offset by (∆x , ∆y ), so views always retain the encoder’s input size.\nEquivariance loss. A naive attraction loss collapses all shifted pairs to identical embeddings\nregardless of shift magnitude. Instead we enforce a target embedding distance proportional to the\nshift magnitude, so that larger shifts produce proportionally more distant embeddings:\n\u0010\n\u00112\n√\n(2)\nLequiv (z1 , z2 , δ) = ∥z1 − z2 ∥ − α d ∥δ̂∥\nwhere z1 is the teacher embedding of x1 and z2 is the student embedding of x2 (see the EMA teacher\nparagraph below), ∥ · ∥ denotes the Euclidean (L2) norm, d is the embedding dimension, δ̂ is the shift\nδ = (∆x , ∆y ) with each component divided by its per-axis maximum\n(∆tmax , ∆pmax ) so that each\n√\ncomponent lies in [0, 1], and α is a scalar hyperparameter. The d factor accounts for concentration\nof measure in high-dimensional spaces, giving α consistent semantic meaning across all hierarchy\nlevels. Unlike a hinge loss, this smooth quadratic both attracts pairs that are too far apart and repels\npairs that are too close. Shifts are sampled from a Beta(2, 2) distribution up to (∆tmax , ∆pmax )\npixels, with ∆pmax = 12 (one octave) and ∆tmax = 48 for the models in this paper.\nChunked SIGReg. SIGReg uses the Epps–Pulley characteristic-function test to enforce an isotropic\nGaussian prior on embeddings [2]. SIGReg computes a tensor of shape (N, Nslices , T ) with Nslices =\n256; on 16 GB GPUs this tensor alone consumed ∼5 GB. We chunk the slice dimension into groups\nof 32 and accumulate in float32:\nP (k)\nLSIGReg ≈ k LSIGReg\n(3)\nwhere k indexes the chunks. This reduces peak VRAM by ∼5 GB and counterintuitively improves\nthroughput by reducing memory-allocator pressure. SIGReg is applied only to student embeddings\nz2 .\nEMA teacher and masked embedding prediction. Following DINO [5] and I-JEPA [1], we\nmaintain an EMA teacher θT ← η θT + (1 − η) θS with η = 0.96, initialised from the student. The\nteacher produces stable target embeddings z1 for the equivariance loss and processes x2 without\nmasking to supply prediction targets for the MEP; a lightweight predictor, conditioned on the student’s\nfull-context representation, predicts the teacher embeddings at randomly masked patch positions. The\nteacher is not redundant with SIGReg: SIGReg alone prevents collapse; the teacher exists to provide\nstable, slowly-varying regression targets. The MEP loss is averaged over hierarchy levels:\nL\n", "numLines": 600, "startLine": 100, "totalLines": 2985}}}
