{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b745a6464a76b829", "type": "tool_result", "content": "700\tLMEP =\n701\t\n702\t1X\n703\têℓ − eTℓ\n704\tL\n705\t\n706\t2\n707\t\n708\tℓ=1\n709\t\n710\twhere eTℓ are the teacher embeddings and êℓ the predictor’s estimates at masked positions.\n711\t10\n712\t\n713\t(4)\n714\t\n715\t\fFigure 5: Soft factorization targets. Differences between pairs of embeddings are encouraged to be\n716\tparallel (t= + 1), anti-parallel (t= − 1), or orthogonal (t=0) depending on augmentation type and\n717\tsign. Directions are not prescribed; only their pairwise geometry is constrained.\n718\t\n719\tFigure 6: Example soft factorization results, Level 0. Left: PCA of normalized embedding difference\n720\tvectors for the three target types, showing the expected geometric relationships. Right: histograms of\n721\tcosine-similarity distributions for each pair type, with means indicated by dashed lines.\n722\t\n723\tSoft factorization of pitch and time. Rather than enforcing pitch/time factorization\n724\tarchitecturally—which prior work has shown loses meaningful cross-dimension interactions [40, 7]—\n725\twe impose a soft geometric constraint through a cosine-similarity loss on augmentation difference\n726\tvectors. The loss is applied only at the three coarsest levels (L0–L2). For a triplet of embeddings za ,\n727\tz1 , z2 , where the anchor za is the embedding of the original unshifted crop and z1 , z2 are embeddings\n728\tof two independently shifted crops of the same excerpt:\n729\td1 = z1 − za ,\n730\t\n731\td2 = z2 − za\n732\t\n733\tLfact = (cos(d1 , d2 ) − t)\n734\t\n735\t2\n736\t\n737\t(5)\n738\t\n739\twhere the target t ∈ {+1, 0, −1} encodes the geometric relationship dictated by the augmentation\n740\ttypes: same-type same-sign shifts should be parallel (t= + 1), same-type opposite-sign shifts antiparallel (t= − 1), and cross-type shifts (one pitch, one time) orthogonal (t=0). No directions are\n741\tprescribed; the system discovers them freely subject to these pairwise constraints. Figure 5 illustrates\n742\tthe three target relationships, and Figures 6–8 show the resulting geometry in a trained encoder.\n743\t\n744\tB\n745\t\n746\tFull STORMBIRD probe results\n747\t\n748\tThe probes of musical qualities used here form a suite of tests called STORMBIRD3 (Sweeping Test\n749\tOf Representational Music Benchmarks, Information Retrieval & Diagnostics). Per-level results for\n750\tthe complete probe suite over all trained encoders (Table A1); the main text’s consolidated table\n751\treports only each model’s best level. Values are reported to 3 decimals.\n752\t3 github.com/drscotthawley/stormbird\n753\t\n754\t11\n755\t\n756\t\fFigure 7: Soft factorization results for levels L1, L2, and L3 (top to bottom), in the format of Figure 6.\n757\t\n758\tTable A1: Encoder roster.\n759\tlabel\n760\t\n761\trun tag\n762\t\n763\tbaseline\n764\t\n765\tc55enc3_vkrDxf\n766\t\n767\tcharacteristics\n768\t\n769\tC55cUL recipe, no auxiliary supervision; 250 epochs,\n770\tPOP909. The unsupervised reference.\n771\tchord-0.5\n772\tchord_ynHbKs\n773\t+ chord heads (root 12-way, quality 4-way) on coarse levels\n774\tL0–L3, λ=0.5; 250 epochs.\n775\tchord-0.1\n776\tchord01b\n777\tChord heads L0–L3, λ=0.1; 250 epochs.\n778\tchord-1.0\n779\tchord10\n780\tChord heads L0–L3, λ=1.0; 250 epochs.\n781\tphrase\n782\tphrase_B2AWUj\n783\t+ phrase-boundary head (1 logit/patch, Dai et al. annotations) on L0–L3, λ=0.5; 250 epochs.\n784\tchall-scr1\n785\tmchordall_screen\n786\tChord heads on ALL levels L0–L5, λ=1.0 (0.167/level after\n787\taveraging); 100-epoch screen.\n788\tchall-scr1.5 mchordall_matched Chord heads on all levels, λ=1.5 = 0.25/level, weightmatched to the coarse-only λ=1.0 arm; 100-epoch screen.\n789\tlakh1x\n790\tlakh1x_DKCVEJ\n791\tBaseline recipe on Lakh512_1x (909 Lakh images); probes\n792\tare cross-domain.\n793\tlakh4x\n794\tlakh4x_qw3nqL\n795\tSame, on the nested 4× Lakh su