onal (t=0). No directions are\nprescribed; the system discovers them freely subject to these pairwise constraints. Figure 5 illustrates\nthe three target relationships, and Figures 6–8 show the resulting geometry in a trained encoder.\n\nB\n\nFull STORMBIRD probe results\n\nThe probes of musical qualities used here form a suite of tests called STORMBIRD3 (Sweeping Test\nOf Representational Music Benchmarks, Information Retrieval & Diagnostics). Per-level results for\nthe complete probe suite over all trained encoders (Table A1); the main text’s consolidated table\nreports only each model’s best level. Values are reported to 3 decimals.\n3 github.com/drscotthawley/stormbird\n\n11\n\n\fFigure 7: Soft factorization results for levels L1, L2, and L3 (top to bottom), in the format of Figure 6.\n\nTable A1: Encoder roster.\nlabel\n\nrun tag\n\nbaseline\n\nc55enc3_vkrDxf\n\ncharacteristics\n\nC55cUL recipe, no auxiliary supervision; 250 epochs,\nPOP909. The unsupervised reference.\nchord-0.5\nchord_ynHbKs\n+ chord heads (root 12-way, quality 4-way) on coarse levels\nL0–L3, λ=0.5; 250 epochs.\nchord-0.1\nchord01b\nChord heads L0–L3, λ=0.1; 250 epochs.\nchord-1.0\nchord10\nChord heads L0–L3, λ=1.0; 250 epochs.\nphrase\nphrase_B2AWUj\n+ phrase-boundary head (1 logit/patch, Dai et al. annotations) on L0–L3, λ=0.5; 250 epochs.\nchall-scr1\nmchordall_screen\nChord heads on ALL levels L0–L5, λ=1.0 (0.167/level after\naveraging); 100-epoch screen.\nchall-scr1.5 mchordall_matched Chord heads on all levels, λ=1.5 = 0.25/level, weightmatched to the coarse-only λ=1.0 arm; 100-epoch screen.\nlakh1x\nlakh1x_DKCVEJ\nBaseline recipe on Lakh512_1x (909 Lakh images); probes\nare cross-domain.\nlakh4x\nlakh4x_qw3nqL\nSame, on the nested 4× Lakh subset (3636 images).\n\n12\n\n\fFigure 8: Soft factorization results for L4 and L5 (top to bottom). The factorization loss is applied\nonly at L0–L2; the geometry visible at L3–L5 is inherited through the shared backbone rather than\ndirectly enforced.\n\nh4\nx\nlak\n\n.530\n.522\n.513\n.543\n.534\n.549\n\n.526\n.532\n.536\n.517\n.528\n.526\n\n.524\n.540\n.548\n.492\n.523\n.524\n\n.516\n.548\n.519\n.510\n.529\n.537\n\n.549\n.536\n.551\n.544\n.555\n.549\n\n.502\n.516\n.544\n.534\n.540\n.532\n\n.539\n.545\n.549\n.548\n.534\n.541\n\n.534\n.538\n.534\n.528\n.533\n.546\n\nh1\nlak\n\nx\n\nr1.\n5\n\n.527\n.526\n.540\n.540\n.540\n.536\n\nall\nch\n\n-sc\n\nr1\nch\n\nall\n-sc\n\nph\n\nL0\nL1\nL2\nL3\nL4\nL5\n\nras\n\nch\n\ne\n\n1.0\n\nch\n\nord\n-\n\nch\nord\n\nord\n-\n\nsel\n\n-0.\n\nLevel\n\nba\n\nine\n\n5\n\n0.1\n\nTable A2: Chord quality accuracy (chance ≈.50) ↑. Here and for all following tables, cells are shaded\nper row with the plasma colormap (yellow = best, honoring each caption’s arrow), and the best value\nin each row is bold.\n\nlak\n\nh4\nx\n\nlak\nh1\n\nx\n\nr1.\n5\nch\nall\n\n-sc\n\nr1\n-sc\nall\nch\n\n.457\n.570\n.594\n.582\n.405\n.242\n\n.090\n.102\n.138\n.212\n.246\n.253\n\n.480\n.555\n.607\n.627\n.442\n.251\n\n.086\n.102\n.118\n.160\n.199\n.221\n\n.229\n.313\n.330\n.328\n.305\n.242\n\n.418\n.541\n.578\n.564\n.563\n.254\n\n.101\n.111\n.132\n.169\n.206\n.220\n\n.106\n.104\n.109\n.161\n.198\n.263\n\ne\nph\nras\n\n1.0\nord\nch\n\n0.1\nord\nch\n\n-0.\n\n.094\n.090\n.107\n.132\n.203\n.243\n\nord\nch\n\nL0\nL1\nL2\nL3\nL4\nL5\n\nsel\n\nLevel\n\nba\n\nine\n\n5\n\nTable A3: Root note accuracy (12-class, chance .083) ↑\n\n13\n\n\fscr\n1.5\n\ncr1\n\nch\nord\n\nph\nras\ne\n\nch\nall\n-s\n\nch\nall\n-\n\nlak\nh\n\n−.019\n−.019\n.064\n.277\n.310\n.646\n\n.686\n.692\n.731\n.826\n.828\n.824\n\n−.020\n.016\n.133\n.380\n.380\n.460\n\n.704\n.733\n.737\n.825\n.835\n.839\n\n−.011\n.019\n.096\n.286\n.509\n.751\n\n.188\n.300\n.444\n.563\n.635\n.726\n\n.676\n.711\n.760\n.823\n.824\n.813\n\n−.006 −.016\n.032\n.004\n.132\n.073\n.396\n.296\n.621\n.424\n.758\n.614\n\nlak\n\nh4\nx\n\nch\nord\n\nL0\nL1\nL2\nL3\nL4\nL5\n\n1x\n\nch\nord\n\n-1.\n0\n\n-0.\n1\n\nLevel\n\nba\nsel\nine\n\n-0.\n5\n\nTable A4: Chroma regression R2 ↑\n\nph\